We will reason about the following diagram to find the perimeter.
[asy]
unitsize(2mm);
defaultpen(linewidth(.7pt));
dotfactor=3;

path[] todraw = (1,9)--(9,9)--(9,1) ^^ (8,9)--(8,8)--(9,8) ^^ (5,9)--(5,8)--(6,8)--(6,9) ^^ (9,5)--(8,5)--(8,6)--(9,6) ^^ (8,8.5)--(6,8.5) ^^ (8.5,8)--(8.5,6) ^^ (5,8.5)--(4,8.5) ^^ (8.5,5)--(8.5,4);
path[] tofill = Circle((1.3,8.5),.15) ^^ Circle((2.1,8.5),.15) ^^ Circle((2.9,8.5),.15) ^^ Circle((8.5,1.3),.15) ^^ Circle((8.5,2.1),.15) ^^ Circle((8.5,2.9),.15);

for(int i = 0; i < 4; ++i)
{
draw(rotate(90*i)*todraw);
fill(rotate(90*i)*tofill);
}
[/asy>
[/think]<answer>Strategically, we’ll notice that a certain property must either be met by all square posts or by none a square post. This means that any instance where a post can either be counted as part of the perimeter or not will not affect the perimeter at all, and will be readily eliminated. In this case, the very top horizontal, particularly the very top rightmost vertical, are all the same, so we can easily divide the polygon into simpler parts. Notice that each outer side of the entire polygon consists of four touching sides of one of the square posts, for a total of \(4*5=20\) of these little spots between posts. This, coupled with the fact all the outer sides are equal, we can list the perimeter in terms of the sum of the top right side and the left side. This gives \(20(15)+21+20=331\). In which we have four little relations, touching sides of five square posts, then one couple of touching sockets of length five plus one touching socket of length six, all with each side touching 4 square posts with a side width of 4, and we then multiply these relations to get the perimeter of the entire field, including an amount from each of the top right and left sides, plus the last couple of touching sockets, from the side opposing the square posts once, to get the final solution to be \(331\) feet\end{answer}

