 First, we note that since $\mathbf{a}$, $\mathbf{b}$, and $\mathbf{p}$ are collinear, all three vectors can be written as scalar multiples of $\mathbf{p}$. So we must have $\mathbf{a} = k \mathbf{p}$ and $\mathbf{b} = \ell \mathbf{p}$, for some constants $k$ and $\ell$. Then using the fact that $\mathbf{p}$ is just $\mathbf{a}$ projected onto $\mathbf{v}$, we have \begin{align*}
\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{p} \cdot \mathbf{v},
\end{align*}but $\mathbf{p} = k \mathbf{a}$, so substituting gives $k \mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{p} \cdot \mathbf{v}$. Then multiplying both sides by $\frac{1}{k}$, we get \begin{align*}
k \mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{p} \cdot \mathbf{v} \iff \mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{p} \cdot \mathbf{v} \iff \mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{v} = k \mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{v}.
\end{align*}Furthermore, in the same way, we can find $\ell$ by just using the fact that $\mathbf{b} \cdot \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{p} \cdot \mathbf{v}$ because of the collinearity. Solving for $k$ and $\ell$ gives us the values in the answer! </think> <answer> $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -\textstyle\frac32 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$ </answer>

